# K0 / B010 — Run 2 replica annotation (blind).

C R245 OL GOV-015,GOV-016 | MUST NOT | Repository-changing Git operations happen only when explicitly requested and parallel work uses separate branches or worktrees; completing an implementation is not a request to commit, push or merge. | Git operations | explicit request
C R246 OL GOV-019 | MUST NOT | Never discard, overwrite, reset, clean or otherwise destroy user work to make a Git operation easier. | Git operations | none stated
C R247 OL GOV-020 | MUST | Before any repository-changing operation inspect the relevant state (git status, git diff, git diff --cached) and use the minimum operation required; never silently clean unrelated working-tree changes. | Git operations | none stated
C R248 OL WF-022 | MUST | Before committing inspect the staged diff and include only the requested logical change; do not stage unrelated modified files or clean them up; prefer one logical change per commit with implementation and its tests together, no artificial tiny commits, no combined unrelated fixes and refactors. | Git commits | none stated
C R249 OL GOV-014,GOV-019 | MUST | Follow the repository's branch-naming convention (defaults feature/, fix/, refactor/, test/, docs/, chore/ with meaningful names) and do not rename an existing user branch to satisfy a convention. | Git branches | none stated
C R250 OL GOV-016,GOV-019 | MUST | Keep each worktree focused on its branch; one branch is not checked out in several normal worktrees; before removing a worktree verify its changes are preserved and never remove one holding uncommitted user work. | Git worktrees | none stated
C R251 OL GOV-020,WF-003,WF-022 | MUST | Before pushing confirm the intended branch and understood working/staged state, ensure relevant verification completed and avoid pushing unrelated local commits. | git push | none stated
C R252 OL GOV-015 | MUST NOT | Never force-push a shared branch unless the user explicitly requests history rewriting and the consequences are understood. | git push | explicit request
C X30 EX GOV-015 | MUST NOT | Treat main, master and other protected or shared branches as non-rewritable. | Git history rewriting | none stated | GOV-015 allows history-changing operations on explicit request; declaring main/master non-rewritable is a stricter, unconditional restriction. | Force-pushing main is treated as forbidden even when requested.
C R253 OL GOV-019,GOV-020,WF-003 | MUST | For merge/rebase follow the repository/team convention or the user's explicit request; inspect local uncommitted work first and never automatically stash, reset or discard it; resolve conflicts from intended combined behavior, not mechanically ours/theirs; run relevant verification afterwards. | git merge/rebase | none stated
C R254 OL WF-003,WF-009,GOV-014 | MUST | Run verify before preparing a PR; one focused purpose without accidental unrelated changes; never claim checks that were not run. | Pull requests | none stated
C X31 EX WF-009 | MUST | A PR describes what changed, why, how it was verified and any compatibility or migration notes. | Pull requests | none stated | No oracle policy fixes PR description content. | PRs without a verification/compatibility description are non-conformant.
C R255 OL GOV-015,GOV-020 | MUST | git reset --hard, git clean, git push --force/--force-with-lease, branch deletion and rebasing shared history are high-risk: run them only when explicitly requested (or required by an approved workflow, see UNCERTAIN-K0-0002) and inspect what will be lost or rewritten first. | Destructive Git operations | see U2
C U2 LU GOV-015 | MAY (exception) | High-risk Git operations may run when 'required by an approved workflow', not only when explicitly requested. | Destructive Git operations | none stated | Open question: GOV-015 requires an explicit request for repository-changing Git operations; whether 'required by an approved workflow' means a workflow the user explicitly requested (entailed) or allows destructive operations without a specific request (relaxation) is not determinable from the text. | Under the broad reading a workflow could reset or force-push without a specific user request.
C R256 OL DEP-011,DEP-020,RUN-015 | DEFAULT | Resilience policies match failure and operation semantics: add a mechanism only for an identified failure scenario, configure it through the Microsoft.Extensions resilience APIs, the standard handler is not mandatory for every client, and handlers with overlapping strategies are not stacked. | Outbound HTTP resilience | none stated
C X32 EX RUN-016,DEP-020 | MUST NOT | Do not wrap normal EF Core operations in a generic application-level retry pipeline; prefer the provider's EF Core execution strategy for transient retry and handle transaction/retry interaction per provider guidance; HTTP retry policies are not database policies. | Database operations | none stated | RUN-016 limits retries to safe operations and DEP-020 sets resilience package defaults; no oracle policy forbids application-level retry pipelines around database calls or prescribes provider execution strategies (the oracle treats database-retry examples as technique, EXC-007). | A resilience pipeline wrapped around repository calls is prohibited.
C R257 OL ARC-006,ARC-003 | MUST NOT | DelegatingHandlers are for genuine HTTP pipeline concerns (authorization header acquisition, correlation/context propagation, request signing), not business decisions. | Outbound HTTP | none stated
C X33 EX RUN-005 | MUST | Per-request values such as dynamic access tokens go on the request or through a handler; mutating a shared client's DefaultRequestHeaders concurrently for request-specific values is a race (stable media-type headers may be configured on the client). | Outbound HttpClient usage | stable headers | RUN-005 governs client lifetime only; no oracle policy restricts how request-specific headers are applied. | Setting a per-user Authorization header on a shared client's DefaultRequestHeaders is treated as a defect.

